Your FORWARD chain is clean and structured properly, but notice in your POSTROUTING table that you still have duplicate and legacy MASQUERADE rules on wg0 (4669 packets hit the first generic one).

Because Cilium's eBPF engine natively handles SNAT via CILIUM_POST_nat / BPF maps, these leftover host-level MASQUERADE rules are redundant.

Let's do a quick final flush of those legacy NAT rules on fedora, verify the clean chain, and then lock the configuration down persistently.

Step 1: Clean Up Stale NAT Rules on Fedora
Run this command on the Fedora node to strip out all non-Cilium MASQUERADE rules targeting wg0:

Bash
sudo iptables -t nat -D POSTROUTING -o wg0 -j MASQUERADE 2>/dev/null || true
sudo iptables -t nat -D POSTROUTING -s 10.32.4.0/24 -o wg0 -j MASQUERADE 2>/dev/null || true
sudo iptables -t nat -D POSTROUTING -s 10.32.0.0/12 -o wg0 -j MASQUERADE 2>/dev/null || true

Re-run until all duplicates are removed:

